= Solution
{parent=i}

Multiply numerator and denominator by the denominator's <complex conjugate>:
$$
\frac{3+2i}{2-3i}=\frac{(3+2i)(2+3i)}{2^2+3^2}
=\frac{6+13i-6}{13}=i.
$$
Thus the <real part> and <imaginary part> are
$$
\boxed{\operatorname{Re}=0,\qquad\operatorname{Im}=1.}
$$

= ii
{parent=2}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=ii}

The <polar form> is $1+i\sqrt3=2e^{i\pi/3}$. By <De Moivre's theorem>,
$$
(1+i\sqrt3)^3=8e^{i\pi}=-8.
$$
Therefore the <real part> and <imaginary part> are
$$
\boxed{\operatorname{Re}=-8,\qquad\operatorname{Im}=0.}
$$

= iii
{parent=2}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=iii}

Using <Euler's formula>, the difference of the two <complex exponentials> is $2i\sin(\pi/4)=i\sqrt2$. Hence
$$
(i\sqrt2)^4=4i^4=4,
\qquad\boxed{\operatorname{Re}=4,\qquad\operatorname{Im}=0.}
$$
These are the <real part> and <imaginary part>, respectively.

