The <normal vectors> are $\mathbf n_A=(1,2,-4)$ and $\mathbf n_B=(2,-1,3)$. Their <dot product> is $-12$, and their <norms> are $\sqrt{21}$ and $\sqrt{14}$. The usual <angle between two planes> is the smaller, unoriented angle, so
$$
\boxed{\theta=\arccos\frac{12}{\sqrt{21}\sqrt{14}}
=\arccos\frac{2\sqrt6}{7}\approx45.6^\circ.}
$$
For the particular signed <normal vectors> printed above, the angle is the supplement $\pi-\theta\approx134.4^\circ$. Reversing either <normal vector> exchanges these two angles without changing either <plane>.

= b
{parent=3}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=b}

A parallel <plane> has the same <normal vector>, so write $C:x+2y-4z=d$. Substitution of the given point gives $d=4+4-4=4$. Thus
$$
\boxed{C:x+2y-4z=4.}
$$
A displacement between the two <planes> has a component of magnitude $|5-4|/\|\mathbf n_A\|$ along their common <unit vector> normal. The <distance between parallel planes> is consequently
$$
\boxed{\operatorname{dist}(A,C)=\frac1{\sqrt{21}}.}
$$

